  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 257 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nThe upper-level ridge will continue to flatten through the week, and \nsurface high pressure will be compressed to our south and east.\nFor the most part, the loss of the protective ridging will only\nallow more high cloud cover to blow into the region. Despite \nthe clouds, low-level warm air advection is expected to pick up \nin the middle of this week as winds veer to southwesterly. NBM \nmax temperature IQR spread yields high confidence in warmer \nhighs on Wednesday and Thursday, as it only reaches 2-3 degrees \ncentered around 78 degrees and 82 degrees respectively. \n\nAt the same time, a shortwave will be traversing the northern \nPlains/upper Midwest, and its surface reflection will be pushing a \ncold front into the Mid-Mississippi Valley. Deterministic guidance \ndisagrees whether or not the front will reach northern Missouri/west-\ncentral Illinois on Thursday morning, or if it will have enough \nmoisture convergence along it to produce light rain. Our better \nchance for rain will come Saturday night into Sunday morning as \nanother shortwave enters the region. This feature is forecast to be \nweakening as it arrives, but to what extent is uncertain. Low-level \nconvergence does not look appreciable either, so a widespread \nrainfall is not expected. NBM and LREF probabilities are still \nconsistent in showing a 30-50% chance of 0.25\" of rain this weekend, \ncorroborating this.",
